## The idea

Every path has one highest node where it **bends**. Through that node its length is height(left) + height(right) edges. So compute every node's height **bottom-up** (post-order) and keep the best left + right. One pass, and an explicit stack avoids deep recursion.

## Step by step

1. Post-order with a stack: push a node, then its children; handle the node only after its children are done.
2. l = left child's height (0 if none), r = right child's height.
3. best = max(best, l + r).
4. height[node] = 1 + max(l, r).

## Worked example

**Input:** tree [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| 4, 5 | leaves → height 1 |
| 2 | l = 1, r = 1 → path 2 edges; height 2 |
| 3 | leaf → height 1 |
| 1 | l = 2, r = 1 → path 3 edges (4-2-1-3) |

**Result:** 3

## Cost

O(n) time and memory.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LinkedIn (reported Oct 2026) — LeetCode 543 Diameter of Binary Tree: edges on the longest path between any two nodes.
// TreeNode is provided by the starter (Models.cs): int Value; TreeNode Left, Right.

public static class BinaryDiameter {
    public static int Of(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return 0;
        // Post-order with an explicit stack: a node is finished after both children, so their heights are known.
        var height = new Dictionary<TreeNode, int>();     // height in nodes: a leaf is 1
        var stack = new Stack<(TreeNode node, bool childrenDone)>();
        stack.Push((root, false));
        int best = 0;
        while (stack.Count > 0) {
            var (node, done) = stack.Pop();
            if (!done) {
                stack.Push((node, true));                 // come back after the children
                if (node.Left != null) stack.Push((node.Left, false));
                if (node.Right != null) stack.Push((node.Right, false));
                continue;
            }
            int l = node.Left == null ? 0 : height[node.Left];
            int r = node.Right == null ? 0 : height[node.Right];
            if (l + r > best) best = l + r;               // the longest path that bends at this node, in edges
            height[node] = 1 + Math.Max(l, r);
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Edges or nodes?**

Edges here; nodes would be one more.

**Q: Through the root?**

Not necessarily — the classic wrong answer only checks the root.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(n) time, O(n) for the heights and stack.

**Q: Why is "height at every node" O(n²)?**

Each height call walks the whole subtree; on a chain that is n + (n−1) + …

**Q: Shared state?**

Read-only tree.

**Q: A general tree given as edges.**

Two BFS passes: the farthest node from anywhere is one end of a diameter — the Agoda question in this bank.

**Q: Related?**

Longest univalue path and binary tree maximum path sum use the same "best bend at each node" idea.
